## Summary

### 1. Dispatch mechanism (`ability_effects.go`)

It's a plain Go `switch` on a **string** (`effect := stackObj.Text`, ability_effects.go:39), **not** an enum/const block and **not** a handler-func map. Near line 19 there is no type/const declaration for effect kinds at all — line 19 is just the top-level entry point:

```go
// ability_effects.go:17-19
// ExecuteAbilityEffect executes a triggered/activated ability effect
// Returns an error if the effect cannot be executed
func (gs *GameState) ExecuteAbilityEffect(stackObj *StackObject) error {
```

The switch itself starts at ability_effects.go:704 (`switch effect {`), with cases matching on raw string literals such as `"earthbend"`, `"damage"`, `"pump"`, etc. — new effect kinds are added purely by adding a new `case "switch_pt":` string, no enum registration needed.

Relevant existing cases (ability_effects.go:815-919), quoted in full:

```go
// ability_effects.go:858-875
case "pump":
    return gs.executePumpEffect(controller, source, effectValue, targets)
case "pump_until_eot":
    // "Target creature gets +N/+N until end of turn" as a sequence/mode
    // atom (modal round 2, 2026-08-02) — executePumpEffect's targeted
    // branch registers exactly this UntilEOT ContinuousEffect. NO-TARGET
    // GUARD: unlike "pump", a pump_until_eot atom is always the printed
    // targeted form (the parser has no self shape for it), so with no
    // target supplied this must stay a no-op — falling into
    // executePumpEffect's self-pump default would silently pump the
    // SOURCE instead of the printed target (the ~180 shipped activated/
    // triggered records whose per-atom target never survives conversion
    // keep their current inert behavior until target promotion exists
    // for them).
    if len(targets) == 0 {
        return nil
    }
    return gs.executePumpEffect(controller, source, effectValue, targets)
case "pump_all":
    ...
```

`pump_all` (ability_effects.go:876-919) builds a filter-scoped `ContinuousEffect` directly inline (with `AffectsFilter`, `AffectsController`, `AffectsSubtype`, `ExcludeSelf`, `AffectsAttacking/Blocking`) rather than calling a helper — showing the two accepted patterns: delegate-to-`execute*Effect` helper (like `pump`) or build the `ContinuousEffect` inline in the case body (like `pump_all`).

### 2. Function signature / access for `pump`

```go
// ability_effects.go:3915
func (gs *GameState) executePumpEffect(controller int, source *Card, effectValue map[string]interface{}, targets []Target) error {
```

Full body (ability_effects.go:3915-3953):

```go
func (gs *GameState) executePumpEffect(controller int, source *Card, effectValue map[string]interface{}, targets []Target) error {
    power := getIntValue(effectValue, "power", 0)
    toughness := getIntValue(effectValue, "toughness", 0)

    // If no targets, might be a self-pump
    if len(targets) == 0 && source != nil && source.IsCreature() {
        // Create temporary effect (until end of turn)
        effect := &ContinuousEffect{
            Source:       source,
            PowerMod:     power,
            ToughnessMod: toughness,
            UntilEOT:     true,
        }
        gs.EffectManager.Register(effect)
        return nil
    }

    // Apply to targets
    for _, target := range targets {
        if target.IsCard {
            card, err := gs.Battlefield.GetCard(target.CardID)
            if err != nil {
                continue
            }
            if card.IsCreature() {
                effect := &ContinuousEffect{
                    Source:          source,
                    AffectsSpecific: card.ID,
                    PowerMod:        power,
                    ToughnessMod:    toughness,
                    UntilEOT:        true,
                }
                gs.EffectManager.Register(effect)
            }
        }
    }

    return nil
}
```

Receiver is `*GameState` (gives access to `gs.Battlefield`, `gs.EffectManager`, `gs.Players`, etc.). Params: `controller int` (acting player index), `source *Card` (the ability's source, used for self-pump default and as `ContinuousEffect.Source`), `effectValue map[string]interface{}` (parsed effect payload, read via `getIntValue`/`getStringValue` helpers), `targets []Target` (resolved targets, each either `Target.IsCard`+`CardID` or `Target.IsPlayer`). There is no separate "duration" param — duration is expressed as a field (`UntilEOT: true`) on the `ContinuousEffect` struct that gets registered with `gs.EffectManager.Register(effect)`. No live game-state pointer is passed beyond `gs` itself, and no explicit "old value" snapshot is taken.

### 3. Power/Toughness computation in `card.go`

```go
// card.go:3066-3090
// GetPowerWithEffects returns effective power including all continuous effects
// Layer order: 7b (set P/T), 7c (+N/+N effects), 7d (counters)
func (c *Card) GetPowerWithEffects(em *EffectManager) int {
    // Animated non-creatures (manlands) have nil BasePower but obtain a set
    // power via a ContinuousEffect. Skip the nil-guard so Layer 7b applies.
    if c.BasePower == nil && !c.AnimatedAsCreature {
        return 0
    }

    base := 0
    if c.BasePower != nil {
        base = *c.BasePower
    }

    if em == nil {
        return base + c.Counters.GetPowerModifier()
    }

    // Check if any effect sets power to a specific value (Layer 7b)
    if setPower := em.GetSetPower(c, c.Controller); setPower != nil {
        return *setPower + em.GetPowerBonus(c, c.Controller) + c.Counters.GetPowerModifier()
    }
    // Normal case: base + bonuses (Layer 7c)
    return base + em.GetPowerBonus(c, c.Controller) + c.Counters.GetPowerModifier()
}

// GetToughnessWithEffects returns effective toughness including all continuous effects
// Layer order: 7b (set P/T), 7c (+N/+N effects), 7d (counters)
func (c *Card) GetToughnessWithEffects(em *EffectManager) int {
    if c.BaseToughness == nil && !c.AnimatedAsCreature {
        return 0
    }

    base := 0
    if c.BaseToughness != nil {
        base = *c.BaseToughness
    }

    if em == nil {
        return base + c.Counters.GetToughnessModifier()
    }

    if setToughness := em.GetSetToughness(c, c.Controller); setToughness != nil {
        return *setToughness + em.GetToughnessBonus(c, c.Controller) + c.Counters.GetToughnessModifier()
    }
    return base + em.GetToughnessBonus(c, c.Controller) + c.Counters.GetToughnessModifier()
}
```

Yes — both methods delegate to an `*EffectManager` (`em`) passed in as a param, computed as: base value, then Layer 7b "set" override (`em.GetSetPower`/`em.GetSetToughness`), then Layer 7c additive bonus (`em.GetPowerBonus`/`em.GetToughnessBonus`), then Layer 7d counters (`c.Counters.GetPowerModifier()`/`GetToughnessModifier()`).

`GetSetPower`/`GetSetToughness` (effects.go:1030-1065) scan `m.effects` for any `ContinuousEffect` whose `SetPower`/`SetToughness` pointer is non-nil and matches the card (via `effectAffectsCard`), returning the value from the effect with the **latest `Timestamp`** (last one registered wins — not additive/combined for the "set" layer).

There are also base-only convenience methods with no effect awareness:
```go
// card.go:3054-3064
func (c *Card) GetPower() int {
    return c.GetBasePower()
}
func (c *Card) GetToughness() int {
    return c.GetBaseToughness()
}
```

### 4. Independent vs. combined read

Power and Toughness are read through **two entirely separate method calls** with no shared/atomic snapshot: `GetPowerWithEffects(em)` and `GetToughnessWithEffects(em)` are independent functions, each independently querying `em.GetSetPower`/`em.GetPowerBonus` or `em.GetSetToughness`/`em.GetToughnessBonus` against the *live* effect list at call time. There is no combined `GetPTWithEffects` that returns both atomically.

This matters for `switch_pt`: since a swap effect must be implemented as a registered `ContinuousEffect` (with `SetPower`/`SetToughness`, mirrored from each other), the values to swap must be captured by calling `card.GetPowerWithEffects(gs.EffectManager)` and `card.GetToughnessWithEffects(gs.EffectManager)` into local variables *before* registering the new swap effect — since `GetSetPower`/`GetSetToughness` both use "latest timestamp wins" semantics, registering the new effect changes what subsequent reads return, so the two reads must happen first, both from the pre-swap state, and only then should the new `ContinuousEffect{SetPower: &oldToughness, SetToughness: &oldPower, UntilEOT: true}`-style effect be registered (whether "current" values at swap-time include Layer 7c bonuses/counters at time of swap is a design decision the ticket needs to make, since `GetSetPower` only returns the *set* component, not bonuses/counters — `GetPowerWithEffects`'s returned int is the fully composed value including counters/bonuses).

### 5. Scope-resolution machinery already visible

`ContinuousEffect` (effects.go:12 onward) already carries generic scope/targeting fields reused by every case in the switch:
- `AffectsSpecific string` — single target card ID (used by `pump`'s targeted branch, ability_effects.go:3942)
- `AffectsFilter`, `AffectsController` (e.g. `"you"` vs. `""` unrestricted), `AffectsSubtype` — used by `pump_all` for "creatures you control" / "each creature" style scopes (ability_effects.go:905-907, 918+)
- `ExcludeSelf bool` — for "other creatures" (ability_effects.go:910-911, `affects_other`)
- `AffectsAttacking` / `AffectsBlocking` — used for "attacking creatures" scope (ability_effects.go:894-899, 913-919)
- Matching is centralized in `m.effectAffectsCard(e, card, sourceControllerIdx)` (referenced in effects.go:1038, 1057), a single predicate consulted by `GetSetPower`, `GetSetToughness`, `GetPowerBonus`, `GetToughnessBonus`, etc.

For "self" vs. "target" resolution at the ability-effect-dispatch layer (not the ContinuousEffect layer), the no-target fallback pattern is: `if len(targets) == 0 && source != nil && source.IsCreature()` → self (as in `executePumpEffect`, ability_effects.go:3920), vs. iterating `targets []Target` (`Target.IsCard`/`CardID`) for explicit targets. There's also a `target_ref == "event_subject"` referent-binding pattern (ability_effects.go:840-844) for atoms that refer back to "that creature" from a triggering event rather than a player-chosen target — an additional scope-resolution path worth knowing about if `switch_pt` might ever need a non-player-chosen-target variant.

